examples.question_text:
The network has edges $JK$, $KL$, $LJ$, $JM$, $MN$ and $NJ$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (-2,1.4);
\coordinate (L) at (-2,-1.4);
\coordinate (M) at (2,1.4);
\coordinate (N) at (2,-1.4);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(J)--(M)--(N)--(J);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[above] {$J$} (K) node[left] {$K$} (L) node[left] {$L$} (M) node[right] {$M$} (N) node[right] {$N$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Classify each route as a walk, trail, path, circuit or cycle. Give every term that applies.
(a) $K\to J\to M\to N$
(b) $K\to J\to M\to N\to J$
(c) $J\to K\to L\to J$
(d) $J\to K\to L\to J\to M\to N\to J$
(e) $K\to J\to K$

There are two separate edges between $U$ and $V$, and a loop at $V$. Find every vertex degree and identify the odd-degree vertices.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (U) at (0,0);
\coordinate (V) at (3,0);
\coordinate (W) at (3,2.5);
\coordinate (X) at (0,2.5);
\draw (U)--(V)--(W)--(X)--(U);
\draw (U) .. controls (1,-1) and (2,-1) .. (V);
\draw (V) .. controls (4.5,-0.7) and (4.5,1) .. (V);
\foreach \v in {U,V,W,X}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (U) node[left] {$U$} (V) node[below] {$V$} (W) node[above right] {$W$} (X) node[above left] {$X$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Count both edges between $U$ and $V$ separately. The loop contributes $2$ to the degree of $V$ because both its ends meet $V$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (U) at (0,0);
\coordinate (V) at (3,0);
\coordinate (W) at (3,2.5);
\coordinate (X) at (0,2.5);
\draw (U)--(V)--(W)--(X)--(U);
\draw (U) .. controls (1,-1) and (2,-1) .. (V);
\draw (V) .. controls (4.5,-0.7) and (4.5,1) .. (V);
\foreach \v in {U,V,W,X}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (U) node[left] {$U\ (3)$} (V) node[below] {$V\ (5)$} (W) node[above right] {$W\ (2)$} (X) node[above left] {$X\ (2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\deg U=3$, $\deg V=5$, $\deg W=2$ and $\deg X=2$.

The odd-degree vertices are $U$ and $V$. Adding a loop changes a degree by $2$, so it preserves whether that degree is odd or even.

examples.question_text:
The network shows four walkways in a park. Can a caretaker use every walkway exactly once and return to the starting junction? Justify your answer and give a suitable route.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (3,0);
\coordinate (L) at (3,2);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$} (K) node[below right] {$K$} (L) node[above right] {$L$} (M) node[above left] {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The network is connected.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (3,0);
\coordinate (L) at (3,2);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J\ (2)$} (K) node[below right] {$K\ (2)$} (L) node[above right] {$L\ (2)$} (M) node[above left] {$M\ (2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Every vertex has degree $2$, so there are no odd-degree vertices. An Eulerian circuit exists.

The caretaker can follow $J\to K\to L\to M\to J$. This uses all four walkways exactly once and returns to the start.

examples.question_text:
A courier must travel along every edge exactly once. Determine whether this is possible, whether the courier can return to the start, and the required endpoints. Then decide which proposed route is an Eulerian trail.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (3,0);
\coordinate (L) at (3,2);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\draw (K)--(M);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$} (K) node[below right] {$K$} (L) node[above right] {$L$} (M) node[above left] {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Route 1: $M\to J\to K\to L\to M\to K$
Route 2: $J\to K\to L\to M\to J$
Route 3: $M\to K\to M\to J\to K\to L$

examples.solution_text:
The network is connected.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (3,0);
\coordinate (L) at (3,2);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\draw (K)--(M);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J\ (2)$} (K) node[below right] {$K\ (3)$} (L) node[above right] {$L\ (2)$} (M) node[above left] {$M\ (3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Exactly two vertices, $K$ and $M$, have odd degree. An Eulerian trail exists, with one of these vertices as its start and the other as its finish. An Eulerian circuit does not exist, so the courier cannot return to the start.

Route 1 uses $MJ$, $JK$, $KL$, $LM$ and $MK$: all five edges exactly once. It is an Eulerian trail from $M$ to $K$.

Route 2 omits $KM$.

Route 3 repeats $KM$ in opposite directions and omits $LM$. Neither route 2 nor route 3 is an Eulerian trail.

examples.question_text:
A technician wants to inspect every connection in this network exactly once in one continuous walk. Is this possible? Explain using the degrees.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (4,0);
\coordinate (L) at (2,3);
\coordinate (M) at (2,1);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(J);
\draw (J)--(M) (K)--(M) (L)--(M);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$} (K) node[below right] {$K$} (L) node[above] {$L$} (M) node[below] {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The network is connected.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (4,0);
\coordinate (L) at (2,3);
\coordinate (M) at (2,1);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(J);
\draw (J)--(M) (K)--(M) (L)--(M);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J\ (3)$} (K) node[below right] {$K\ (3)$} (L) node[above] {$L\ (3)$} (M) node[below] {$M\ (3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
All four vertices have odd degree. An Eulerian trail permits at most two odd-degree vertices; an Eulerian circuit requires none.

Neither exists, so the inspection cannot be completed in the requested walk.

examples.question_text:
The connected network consists of a triangle and two edges leading to $T$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (2,0);
\coordinate (R) at (1,2);
\coordinate (S) at (3,2);
\coordinate (T) at (4.5,0.5);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(P);
\draw (R)--(S)--(T);
\foreach \v in {P,Q,R,S,T}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$} (Q) node[below right] {$Q$} (R) node[above] {$R$} (S) node[above] {$S$} (T) node[right] {$T$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the smallest number of extra edges needed to permit an Eulerian circuit. State where to add them and explain why.

examples.solution_text:
$\deg P=2$, $\deg Q=2$, $\deg R=3$, $\deg S=2$ and $\deg T=1$. The odd-degree vertices are $R$ and $T$.

An Eulerian circuit requires every degree to be even. Adding an edge between $R$ and $T$ raises both odd degrees by $1$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutionBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (2,0);
\coordinate (R) at (1,2);
\coordinate (S) at (3,2);
\coordinate (T) at (4.5,0.5);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(P);
\draw (R)--(S)--(T);
\draw[solutionBlue] (R) .. controls (2.5,0.7) and (3.3,0.3) .. (T);
\foreach \v in {P,Q,R,S,T}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (P) node[below left] {$P\ (2)$} (Q) node[below right] {$Q\ (2)$} (R) node[above] {$R\ (4)$} (S) node[above] {$S\ (2)$} (T) node[right] {$T\ (2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The blue edge is the new connection. The network remains connected and every degree is now even.

Zero extra edges would leave two odd vertices, so the smallest number is $1$, joining $R$ and $T$.

examples.question_text:
In the Königsberg bridges problem, vertices represent land masses and edges represent bridges. Explain why no walk can cross all seven bridges exactly once. Then find the smallest number of extra bridges needed to permit an Eulerian trail, give one possible new connection and identify the resulting endpoints.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (U) at (0,2);
\coordinate (V) at (0,-2);
\coordinate (W) at (2,0);
\coordinate (X) at (5,0);
\draw (U) .. controls (0.3,0.9) and (1,0.2) .. (W);
\draw (U) .. controls (1.1,1.8) and (1.8,1.1) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (0.3,-0.9) and (1,-0.2) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (1.1,-1.8) and (1.8,-1.1) .. (W);
\draw (W)--(X);
\draw (U) .. controls (2,3) and (4.5,2) .. (X);
\draw (V) .. controls (2,-3) and (4.5,-2) .. (X);
\foreach \v in {U,V,W,X}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (U) node[left] {$U$} (V) node[left] {$V$} (W) node[above right] {$W$} (X) node[right] {$X$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$\deg U=3$, $\deg V=3$, $\deg W=5$ and $\deg X=3$. All four land masses have odd degree.

At an intermediate land mass, arrival and departure use bridges in pairs. Only the start and finish can have an unpaired bridge, so a walk using every bridge exactly once permits at most two odd vertices. The original network has four, so no such walk exists.

Add one bridge between $U$ and $V$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutionBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (U) at (0,2);
\coordinate (V) at (0,-2);
\coordinate (W) at (2,0);
\coordinate (X) at (5,0);
\draw (U) .. controls (0.3,0.9) and (1,0.2) .. (W);
\draw (U) .. controls (1.1,1.8) and (1.8,1.1) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (0.3,-0.9) and (1,-0.2) .. (W);
\draw (V) .. controls (1.1,-1.8) and (1.8,-1.1) .. (W);
\draw (W)--(X);
\draw (U) .. controls (2,3) and (4.5,2) .. (X);
\draw (V) .. controls (2,-3) and (4.5,-2) .. (X);
\draw[solutionBlue] (U) .. controls (-1.3,1) and (-1.3,-1) .. (V);
\foreach \v in {U,V,W,X}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (U) node[above left] {$U\ (4)$} (V) node[below left] {$V\ (4)$} (W) node[above right] {$W\ (5)$} (X) node[right] {$X\ (3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The blue bridge makes $U$ and $V$ even. The network is connected and only $W$ and $X$ remain odd, so an Eulerian trail exists between $W$ and $X$, in either direction.

The original network does not permit the walk, and one extra bridge does. The minimum is $1$.

examples.question_text:
Every vertex in this network has degree $2$. Explain why there is no Eulerian circuit covering the whole network. If extra edges may be added, including separate edges between the same pair of vertices, find the smallest number needed to permit such a circuit and show where they could go.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (2,0);
\coordinate (L) at (1,1.8);
\coordinate (M) at (4,0);
\coordinate (N) at (6,0);
\coordinate (P) at (5,1.8);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(J);
\draw (M)--(N)--(P)--(M);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N,P}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$} (K) node[below] {$K$} (L) node[above] {$L$} (M) node[below] {$M$} (N) node[below right] {$N$} (P) node[above] {$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The network has two separate components. A walk along edges cannot travel from one triangle to the other, so there is no Eulerian circuit covering every edge, despite the even degrees.

One edge between $K$ and $M$ would connect the components, but it would give both vertices degree $3$. This would permit an open Eulerian trail, not an Eulerian circuit.

Add two separate edges between $K$ and $M$ instead.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutionBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (2,0);
\coordinate (L) at (1,1.8);
\coordinate (M) at (4,0);
\coordinate (N) at (6,0);
\coordinate (P) at (5,1.8);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(J);
\draw (M)--(N)--(P)--(M);
\draw[solutionBlue] (K)--(M);
\draw[solutionBlue] (K) .. controls (2.6,-0.9) and (3.4,-0.9) .. (M);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N,P}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J\ (2)$} (K) node[below, yshift=-12mm] {$K\ (4)$} (L) node[above] {$L\ (2)$} (M) node[below, yshift=-12mm] {$M\ (4)$} (N) node[below right] {$N\ (2)$} (P) node[above] {$P\ (2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The blue edges connect the components and raise both affected degrees to $4$. All degrees are even, so an Eulerian circuit exists.

Zero or one extra edge cannot satisfy both requirements. The smallest number is $2$.
